{"t":1795302827,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"4d591def-463a-4cb6-8e4e-8163eba1b550","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-21T19:49:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 211949\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n249 PM EST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nThe latest forecast trends keep low pressure a bit further\noffshore Sunday, keeping rain amounts limited over the area. The\nonset of rain Sunday also looks to be a bit later. Another area\nof low pressure looks to bring wet and possibly windy weather \nfor the Thanksgiving holiday.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Coastal low to bring increased rain chances on Sunday.\n\n- 2) Cooler temperatures next week with precipitation arriving\n  for the Thanksgiving holiday.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Coastal low to bring increased rain chances on \nSunday.\n\nThe current setup at the surface features high pressure\nretreating into New England. To the south, an inverted trough \nremains along the southeastern U.S. coastline while a wavy \nfrontal zone stretches across central Florida to the north of \nthe Bahamas. Over the next 12 to 24 hours, the persistent ridge\npulls into the Canadian Maritimes. At the same time, the \nfrontal system around Florida is slated to lift northward, \ngenerally paralleling the Atlantic coastline. How close the \naccompanying wave(s) of embedded low pressure track will \ndictate the level of local impacts to the region. The latest\ntrend is for several disorganized low centers to consolidate\ninto a more cohesive system a bit further out to sea.\n\nWinds are gradually shifting to easterly/onshore as the \ninverted coastal trough approaches. Any rain chances look to \nhold off until late tonight, perhaps longer given dry low-level \nprofiles to start.\n\nLatest 12Z guidance has come into better agreement keeping the\nsteadiest rain east of the area. Will continue to forecast a \nbroad 0.10 to 0.25 inches over the area (perhaps slightly less\nin the Shenandoah Valley), perhaps accompanied by a few pockets\napproaching 0.50 inches toward the Chesapeake Bay. With a more \noffshore track of the series of lows, the strength of north to \nnortheasterly winds remains limited, as well. Outside of the \nrain chances, high temperatures will mainly be in the mid 50s to\nmid 60s on Sunday.\n\nThe forecast looks dry for the start of the new work week as an\nupstream cold front moves through early Monday. A downsloping \nnorthwesterly wind will still help raise temperatures well into \nthe 60s. This also produces a favorable upslope flow to produce \nsome light showers along/west of the Allegheny Front. East of\nthe mountains, waves of stratocumulus clouds are possible. Wind\ngusts may persist at times into Monday night behind a secondary\ncold front that ushers in much cooler air.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler temperatures next week with precipitation \narriving for the Thanksgiving holiday.\n\nBehind multiple cold fronts, temperatures on Tuesday cool and return \nto late-November normals throughout the week. High pressure builds \nover the Ohio River Valley giving our area a brief break in \nrainfall until Wednesday evening.\n\nModels are in good agreement for an elongated trough to dig into the \nMississippi River Valley Wednesday into Thursday. This sets up\nanother coastal low pressure system to trail up the Mid-Atlantic \ncoastline by Thanksgiving. This far out, both deterministic and \nensemble guidance show a wide range of potential solutions, so it's \nfar too early to get into specific details. Most of the solutions \ndiffer in how far this system will pass off the coast. A closer \ntrack would increase precipitation amounts and lead to gustier \nwinds. At higher elevations, mixed wintry precipitation remains a \npossibility Thanksgiving Day, especially across the Alleghenies.\nIf this were to occur, snowfall amounts remain modest with \nrecent guidance trending rainier.\n\nUnless a deeper closed low develops, this system advances into \nthe Atlantic by Friday. High pressure developes in the wake of this \nsystem, bringing seasonable, dry conditions for Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /19Z SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nVFR conditions are likely to persist through early Sunday\nmorning, with a gradual lowering and thickening of clouds. \nAn easterly onshore wind this evening will become north to\nnortheast tonight through Sunday. Any restrictions from \napproaching showers should hold until Sunday.\n\nMost aviation models suggest ceilings right along the fringes of\nMVFR and VFR (3,000 feet or so). So any sub-VFR conditions will\nlikely be intermittent and be guided by how much rainfall tracks\nthrough (which has trended less). Winds on Sunday are expected \nto be out of the north to northeasterly direction, with the \nstrongest gusts likely east of the local airspace. Any rain \nchances diminish late Sunday night, though some patchy fog may\nform. A cold front yields improving conditions for Monday,\nthough with waves of VFR stratocu likely. This comes with \nprevailing northwesterlies that gust to around 15 to 20 knots \nduring the afternoon and into the night.\n\nVFR conditions are likely Tuesday, although northwest winds may \nremain a bit gusty. Clouds may increase Wednesday ahead of coastal \nlow pressure, but rain and sub-VFR conditions likely hold off until \nWednesday night.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nExpect modestly increasing east to northeasterly winds into \nSunday as a wavy frontal zone approaches from the coastal \nCarolinas. Given a more eastward track, the strongest winds may \nstay east of the Chesapeake Bay. Brief periods of gusts 15-20\nknots are possible over the mid bay, but confidence in duration\nis too low for an SCA at the moment. A cold front then tracks \nin from the west early Monday with northwesterlies in the wake. \nThe real uptick does not take place until Monday evening/night \nwhich will likely warrant Small Craft Advisories.\n\nPotentially weaker winds Tuesday with Small Craft Advisory \nconditions remaining a possibility in northerly flow. Winds \nfurther increase Wednesday becoming northeasterly overnight as \ncoastal low pressure develops.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nA much moister environment moves into the entire region on Sunday\nas an area of low pressure lifts up the Carolina coast. Winds \npick up intensity early next week behind a strong cold front. \nThis brings in cooler weather but also another round of reduced \ndaily humidities. Another coastal system may bring wet and windy \nconditions to the region by the middle of next week, which may \ninclude a chance for mountain snow. This looks to continue into \nthe Thanksgiving holiday, but uncertainty is high at this time.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nRelatively light winds through Monday may allow for tides to\nelevate somewhat, resulting in action stages at vulnerable sites\nlike Annapolis particularly late Monday afternoon. Thereafter,\nstrong offshore flow will result in decreasing tides Monday\nnight into Tuesday.\n\nAnother coastal low pressure area will likely move up the East\nCoast Wednesday into Thursday. Depending on the evolution and\ntrack, tide levels may rise once again.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...BRO/DHOF/KVH\nAVIATION...BRO/DHOF/KVH\nMARINE...BRO/DHOF/KVH\nFIRE WEATHER...LWX\nTIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...LWX\n"},"forecast":{"t":1795302779,"days":{"2026-11-21":{"hi":null,"lo":52,"pop":13,"short":"Cloudy"},"2026-11-22":{"hi":64,"lo":52,"pop":81,"short":"Light Rain"},"2026-11-23":{"hi":68,"lo":38,"pop":1,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-24":{"hi":53,"lo":32,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-25":{"hi":51,"lo":42,"pop":68,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-26":{"hi":52,"lo":41,"pop":73,"short":"Light Rain 